      "content": "I apologize for the momentary lapse. Stabilization of a volatile Li-ion cell requires a focused approach. To stabilize the cell, follow these steps:\n\n6. **Stabilization**: If the temperature is still rising, apply a thermal management system, such as a thermal interface material (TIM) or a phase-change material, to the affected area. This will help to absorb and dissipate excess heat. Additionally, consider applying a thermal paste or a conductive thermal interface material to the battery's internal components to enhance heat dissipation.\n\nIt's essential to note that Li-ion cells are sensitive to over-discharge, so ensure the battery is not subjected to any further discharge cycles. If the temperature continues to rise, consider disconnecting the battery from the power source and applying a protective covering to prevent further thermal runaway.\n\nIn the event of a thermal runaway, the primary goal is to prevent the cell from reaching its critical temperature. This is often referred to as the \"thermal runaway threshold,\" which is typically around 120°C to 150°C, depending on the cell chemistry and design.",
